{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "בכרך", :translit "bekhorkha", :gloss "your firstborn"} {:he "עשו", :translit "esav", :gloss "Esau"} {:he "בכרך", :translit "bekhorkha", :gloss "your firstborn"} {:he "אנכי הורג את בנך בכרך", :translit "anochi horeg et binkha bekhorkha", :gloss "I am killing your son your firstborn"} {:he "אני", :translit "ani", :gloss "I"} {:he "בני בכורי ישראל", :translit "beni bekhori yisrael", :gloss "My son, My firstborn, Israel"} {:he "עשו", :translit "esav", :gloss "Esau"} {:he "בנך בכורך", :translit "binkha bekhorkha", :gloss "your son your firstborn"} {:he "הנני הורג את בנך בכורך", :translit "hineni horeg et binkha bekhorkha", :gloss "Here I am, killing your son your firstborn"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Interpretive wordplay pivoting on the suffix of בכרך: Jacob is called בכורי (My firstborn) at Exodus 4:22, while Esau is called בכורך (your firstborn) at Genesis 27:19, 32"}], :frame {:text "God declares Israel His firstborn at Exodus 4:22, 'My son, My firstborn, Israel.' Jacob, speaking to Isaac, exploits the suffix of the word בכרך (your firstborn): the bare term 'firstborn' without the second-person suffix is reserved for Israel at Exodus 4:22, while 'your firstborn' points to Esau. The Ba'al HaTurim reads Jacob as saying: I am the one called God's firstborn; but Esau is your firstborn — so when I say בכרך, I mean I am effectively displacing and eliminating your firstborn.", :sources ["Exodus 4:22" "Genesis 27:19"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Interpretive wordplay pivoting on the suffix of בכרך: Jacob is called בכורי (My firstborn) at Exodus 4:22, while Esau is called בכורך (your firstborn) at Genesis 27:19, 32", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:08:37.163133Z", :model "glm-5.2"}